[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2821412534","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/105530#issuecomment-2821412534","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/105530","id":2821412534,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6oK1a2","user":{"login":"Calinou","id":180032,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4MDAzMg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/180032?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou","html_url":"https://github.com/Calinou","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-04-22T13:53:38Z","updated_at":"2025-04-22T13:55:22Z","body":"Why was the issue closed? It still seems to be valid.\n\n> The second issue is that when a material's sampling mode is set to \"Nearest Mipmap Anisotropic,\" it looks the same as \"Linear Mipmap Anisotropic.\" On the other hand, in the OpenGL renderer, it looks the same as \"Nearest Mipmap,\" which is a less unexpected result.\n\nOn Vulkan, nearest mipmap anisotropic filtering is only supported on NVIDIA due to a graphics driver limitation as I understand it. It's not as strict in OpenGL though, as you can see. It's one of those things that modern graphics APIs are worse at doing 🙁 \n\nThe only workaround is to reimplement nearest-neighbor filtering in a shader while still using bilinear filtering, similar to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/88650. This adds some overhead though.","author_association":"MEMBER","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2821412534/reactions","total_count":2,"+1":2,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2933112953","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/105530#issuecomment-2933112953","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/105530","id":2933112953,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6u08B5","user":{"login":"belzecue","id":1931303,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5MzEzMDM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1931303?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue","html_url":"https://github.com/belzecue","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/belzecue/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-06-03T02:04:31Z","updated_at":"2025-06-03T02:11:55Z","body":"I experienced this recently on my two old Intel NUCs (Linux and Windows). See #107053.\n\nKhronos discussion [here](https://github.com/KhronosGroup/Vulkan-Docs/issues/1361) about the problem with Intel driver and anisotropy.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://ap